Luis Miquilena, also  known as Miquilena Martínez   (1853/54 –  September   14, 1945), was a Cuban general, lawyer, journalist and  politician. He fought against American imperialism and the  re-election of President Alfredo Zayas, under whom he    served as Interior.

## Birth and youth[edit | edit source]

Miquilena was born  in Guantánamo.^{[1]} After completing his secondary education^{[1]} and undergraduate studies  at  the University of Havana^{[2]},  he obtained the degree  of Bachelor of Laws in 1882. In  1892, he  obtained a  doctorate and he had participated in the revolution for the country's independence.^{[1]} From 1896 to 1898, he was director and member of the editorial board of  the nationalist paper La Alarma.^{[3]}

## Politics[edit |  edit source]

In  1901,  he was named Secretary of the Interior,  with  his brother  José Antonio de  Miquilena as Secretary of Public Instruction.^{[3]}  During  the Platt Amendment  negotiations, he  represented the Cuban  government.  In the Constituent Assembly  elected  in 1902, Miquilena  was named a member of the  Committee drafting the Constitution.^{[3]}

